<Base title="Read the evidence — Suricata and Cernity, record by record" description="Learn to interpret actual Suricata events and Cernity findings side by side, including seven saved scenarios, original JSON records, investigation steps, and limitations.">
  <section class="hero page-hero"><span class="pill">CERNITY / AN ANALYST'S FIELD GUIDE</span><h1>Read the record.<br />Understand the finding.</h1><p class="lede">What did the sensor actually observe? What did Cernity infer? Follow seven saved test scenarios, compare the records, and learn what to investigate before calling something a threat.</p></section>
  <section class="proof-primer"><div><span class="eyebrow">BEFORE YOU BEGIN</span><h2>A log entry is not<br />automatically an alert.</h2><p>A <strong>flow event</strong> describes a connection. An <strong>alert</strong> records a rule match. A <strong>finding</strong> is a detection system's assessment. A <strong>SIEM document</strong> is the stored record of any of those things.</p><a class="text-link" href="/docs/reading-records/">Learn the vocabulary and field names →</a></div><div class="proof-paths"><div><span class="eyebrow">BASELINE PATH</span><p>Suricata → raw EVE records → SIEM</p><span>Individual observations and signature alerts.</span></div><div><span class="eyebrow">CERNITY PATH</span><p>Same EVE input → Cernity → SIEM</p><span>Derived findings, including revisions and unsuccessful enrichment.</span></div><p>This benchmark retained raw baseline telemetry for comparison. In your deployment, raw Suricata logs appear in the SIEM only if you configure that ingestion path. Cernity's findings forwarder does not forward all raw EVE records.</p></div></section>
  <nav class="case-index" aria-label="Evidence case studies">{cases.map((c,i)=><a href={`#${c.id}`}><span class="mono">0{i+1}</span><span>{c.label}</span><span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a>)}</nav>
  <p class="proof-intro-note"><a href="/proof/detection/">Detection coverage — live Suricata-vs-Cernity captures across every detector →</a><br /><a href="#optional-integrations">nDPI, JA3/JA4, reverse DNS, SLIPS, and optional integration evidence →</a><br />Historical synthetic replays, not live customer incidents. The panels show exported document bodies rather than screenshots of a particular SIEM. Field presentation and search wrappers vary by SIEM.</p>
  {cases.map((c,index)=><ProofCase c={c} index={index}/>)}
  <IntegrationEvidence />
  <section id="methodology" class="proof-methodology"><span class="pill">METHOD & PROVENANCE</span><h2>Where these records came from.</h2><div class="method-grid"><div><h3>What was checked</h3><p>The selected records come from saved pipeline exports. During preparation of this page, every listed export-file SHA-256 in the five primary cases was checked against its bundle manifest. The two older DNS cases lack those manifests and are labeled as lower assurance. Baseline records were compared with source EVE as multisets, ignoring only the SIEM-added <code>@timestamp</code>.</p><p>Counts were recalculated for the specified source, destination, and detector. The selected document bodies retain their original fields and values; the short panels select fields and decode <code>entities</code> only.</p></div><div><h3>What remains private</h3><p>The complete captures, reports, and evidence bundles are not published here. Downloads contain selected synthetic record bodies, all matching Cernity findings, scoped counts, replay offsets, and source export hashes. They are not the full input dataset.</p><p>The hashes document provenance and local verification. Without the original bundles, a visitor cannot independently reproduce every full-run count from these downloads alone.</p></div></div><h3>Completion and clocks</h3><p><code>reconciled</code> is the saved harness's completed accounting state. <code>inputs_drained</code> means the inputs had drained but full lifecycle and delivery reconciliation was not established. A finding with <code>state: FINAL</code> can still have <code>enrichment_state: TIMEOUT</code>; those are separate concerns.</p><p>Replay re-anchors old capture timestamps. Each download preserves its recorded offset. For a source flow, compare <code>flow.start + replay_offset_seconds</code> with the replayed observation window. Do not use <code>@timestamp</code> ingestion values to calculate original connection intervals.</p></section>
  <section id="limitations" class="caveat"><h3>What these comparisons can support</h3><p>The saved records show additional pattern analysis and signature consolidation, alongside false leads and incomplete enrichment. They do not establish general precision, recall, throughput, analyst workload reduction, operating cost, or superiority to equivalent SIEM analytics. A configured SIEM could derive similar patterns from raw telemetry.</p><a class="text-link" href="/docs/analyst-workflow/">Continue with the analyst investigation guide →</a></section>
</Base>
